  <div class="eyebrow">boring-ui · plan · 10 Aug 2026</div>
  <h1>The plan on one page</h1>
  <p class="sub">Eleven frameworks surveyed, nine research reports, one codebase audit. This is what
    survives as work.</p>

  <h2><span class="k">01</span>The finding</h2>
  <p>Session state has <strong>three owners</strong> — pi's transcript, the live pi session, our replay
    buffer — and the snapshot fabricates a cursor with <code>Math.max(persisted.seq, liveSeq)</code>.
    About <strong>3,700 lines</strong> exist to manage that disagreement.</p>
  <p>Flue, which uses the same harness, solved it: pi gets an in-memory <code>messages</code> array and
    <em>never persists</em>; a <code>ConversationRecordWriter</code> is injected; their record stream is the
    only truth. Read from their source. It is not a proposal — it is a working system.</p>

  <h2><span class="k">02</span>The lanes</h2>
  <div class="tw">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Lane</th><th>Contains</th><th>Deletes</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="l">L0 · Decide</td><td>Canonical record schema · Decision 31 · first-party vs third-party agents</td><td>—</td><td><span class="st go">start now</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L1 · Durability</td><td>Canonical writer; pi in-memory + injected writer; settlements + incarnation; per-session store; admit-before-work</td><td>the reconciliation layer</td><td><span class="st wait">needs L0</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L2 · Wire</td><td>Opaque cursors · one read route · implicit sessions (#979) · <code>final</code> authoritative · one command channel · queue-as-state</td><td>client cursor arithmetic</td><td><span class="st wait">needs L1</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L3 · Human intention</td><td>Durable pause · approval as one-shot capability · channel-answerable</td><td>—</td><td><span class="st go">in flight</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L4 · pi convergence</td><td>Stop reimplementing transcript, compaction, skill parsing, tool schemas, truncation, diff</td><td>pure deletion</td><td><span class="st wait">needs L1</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L5 · Observability</td><td><code>observe()</code> runtime stream; metering as subscriber; OTel <em>redacted by default</em></td><td>metering coupling</td><td><span class="st wait">needs L1</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L6 · Authoring DX</td><td>Declarative composition · frozen prompt + signals · compaction config · one-shot run command</td><td>—</td><td><span class="st stop">defer</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="l">L7 · Governance</td><td>Entitlement discovery + revocation · model-hidden arguments · config versioned per session</td><td>—</td><td><span class="st stop">defer</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="dep">L0 ─┬─ L1 ─┬─ L2
    │       ├─ L4
    │       └─ L5
    ├─ L3   (needs only L0's record shape)
    ├─ L6   independent
    └─ L7   independent</div>

  <h2><span class="k">03</span>L0 — the schema everything waits on</h2>
  <p>Eleven frameworks agree on what a durable agent record must carry. This is the intersection.</p>
<pre><code>submission   id · session · tenant/workspace · agent_type · payload
             admitted_at            -- written BEFORE any model work
             idempotency_key?
             status                 -- queued | running | settled
             outcome                -- completed | failed | aborted   (exactly one)
             incarnation            -- which process generation served it

record       session · cursor (opaque) · kind · payload · recorded_at
             one append-only stream per session, one store per session

pause        request_id · session · kind (approval | question)
             action + canonical_args   -- what is being consented to
             state · answered_at · answer
             -- stale answers never authorize the original call</code></pre>
  <div class="box">
    <p><strong>Three rules that make it work</strong> — admit durably before any model work;
      exactly one terminal outcome per submission; <strong>at-least-once execution over exactly-once
      recording</strong>. An interrupted side effect is surfaced as unknown, never silently retried.</p>
  </div>

  <h2><span class="k">04</span>What we keep</h2>
  <p>The audit's do-not-delete list is long and it is all one thing: <strong>governance</strong>.
    Tenant checks, scope verification on every operation, MCP grants, lease fences, workspace path
    authority, submitter identity carried into tool calls, host durable-root selection.</p>
  <div class="box plain">
    <p>Of eleven frameworks, only two ship a tenancy model — Mastra and LangGraph Platform — and both
      paywall it. <strong>Tenancy is not neglected by the field; it is what the field charges for.</strong>
      Our capability model beats both on structural confinement and loses on entitlement discovery and
      central revocation. That gap is L7.</p>
  </div>

  <h2><span class="k">05</span>The honest minimum</h2>
  <p>None of L0–L7 ships a vertical agent. They are well-specified platform work, and platform work is
    self-justifying — every item is defensible, sequenced, and blocks something else.</p>
  <div class="box">
    <p><strong>Do L0 + L3. Stop there.</strong> Decide the record shape, land human intention on it
      — that is the lane with an external driver and work already in flight — and hold the rest until a
      paying vertical asks for a specific piece.</p>
  </div>

  <h2><span class="k">06</span>Open questions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>First-party agents only, or third-party authored?</strong> Decides whether isolation-per-agent
      ever matters, and therefore whether celld is ever relevant. Unanswered.</li>
    <li>Does <code>message-end.final</code> cover one message or a whole turn? Blocks part of L2.</li>
    <li>Can a tool result arrive after the authoritative final? Blocks the reducer simplification.</li>
    <li>Does pi expose selective follow-up cancellation publicly? Blocks one compat shim's removal.</li>
    <li>Do any flag-enabled deployments hold records recoverable only from the current event store?
      Blocks the store migration.</li>
  </ul>

